    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Proved with NO case analysis on the integers at all, which is the point worth recording. `Int.le` is a four-case definition, so the structural proof is sixteen branches over an `Int.add` that is itself stuck in half of them. Instead this route proves `a <= b` iff `b = a + Int.ofNat i` for some natural `i` (`Int.le_dest` forward, `Int.le_ofNat_add` back), and then the law is one ring rearrangement: `(a+i) + (c+j) = (a+c) + (i+j)`. `Int.rec` never appears in the proof term. `formal.statement` is `Kernel::render_lean` of the admitted type, verbatim from `int_theorem_inventory`.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
